>1) What's the deal with statues?  I've heard they contain spellbooks,
>but the only thing I've been able to do with them (besides pick them up)
>is shatter them into a pile of rocks with a pick-axe.

There aren't spellbooks in every statue.  The chance goes up the further
into the dungeon you penetrate.  Keep trying.  As somebody is bound to
point out, you can use statues to #force locks on chests, boxes, etc. 
I've always found that booting them is much more satisfying, though.

>2) What good is Wizard mode?  I mean, it's real nice that it saved my
>life for me in my infinite foolishness, but I got the impression I could
>do more.  How?

It really is an exploration mode more than anything.  People use it to
check out the more esoteric features of the game.  Note that the Mac
version (at the very least) also has an 'exploration mode' that starts
you with a wand of wishing and the ability to prevent yourself from
dying.  Sort of a watered down wizard mode.

>3) I was using an elf (w/ an elven sword of course) to #force a lock on
>a chest I'd found.  I tried numerous times, but all it said was
>something like "You hear a squeaking noise."  What does this mean?

Geez, search me.  My guess would be that the chest is already unlocked,
and you're trying to #force it anyway.  Just a guess, though.

>4) I killed myself by starvation twice already looking for a shop on a
>level which told me I could hear guards marching and money being
>counted.  I searched every wall at least twice, with an elf no less.  Is
>there something I'm missing here?

What you're dealing with is a vault, not a shop.  You're not going to
find a door to the vault.  It is a completely separate room with not
real entrances.  On many levels, however, there will be a teleportation
trap that will pop you into the vault.  When you find yourself in the
vault, you can rob it, but a guard will drop by presently and ask you
your name.  Give it the right response, and you're off scott free. 
'Course, you'll have to make your own way out of the vault, but that is
why dwarves were put in nethack, right?  If you give the wrong response
(I usually make a typo), you'll have to drop your gold and follow the
guard (recommended for wimpy characters), fight (good for tought Chaotic
characters with an altar handy), or motor (teleportation works wonders).

In an entirely different message, an entirely different person asks:

>	I just got this message on a level where I robbed a treasure room:
>
>	"You hear someone searching."
>
>	What does that mean?

Not surprisingly, this is a direct result of your robbing the vault. 
The "You hear someone searching" means that the guard is looking for
you, and will try to nail your sorry butt to the wall if he finds you. 
The guard isn't that tough, though, so don't get too concerned unless
you are a 98 pound weakling.

